{"primaryContentSections":[{"content":[{"type":"heading","anchor":"Overview","text":"Overview","level":2},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Creating a subclass of a Godot type involves a few steps:"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"orderedList","items":[{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Subclassing an existing Godot type with the usual Swift syntax."}]}]},{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Registering your type with Godot, so users can create instances of it","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"and configure it from the user interface."}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Augment the type with your own custom behaviors.","type":"text"}]}]},{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Expose your properties, methods and signals to the Godot Editor.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]}]},{"text":"Subclassing a Godot Type","type":"heading","level":2,"anchor":"Subclassing-a-Godot-Type"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"To subclass a Godot type, you would follow the usual Swift idiom to subclass,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"and annotate it with the @Godot macro, like this:","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"codeListing","syntax":"swift","code":["@Godot","class MySprite: Sprite {","    var timePassed: Double = 0","","    override func _process (delta: Double) {","        time_passed += delta","    ","        var newPos = Vector2(x: Float (10 + (10 * sin(time_passed * 2.0))),","                             y: Float (10.0 + (10.0 * cos(time_passed * 1.5))))","    ","        self.position = newPos","    }","}"]},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"The ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Godot"},{"text":" macro does a few things, it creates a default constructor that","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"follows the convention to call the parent ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"init()"},{"type":"text","text":" method and performs any"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"registrations that you might have done in your class for variables or methods."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"As you will see later, the @Godot macro is applied to a class definition, and"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"will scan your type for various other macros to integrate with Godot.   These"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"attributes will not work if you attempt to apply those in a Swift"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"extension-method, as the @Godot macro has no visibility into those.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"text":"Register Your Type","type":"heading","level":2,"anchor":"Register-Your-Type"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Now we need to tell Godot about the existence of your type, to do this you need","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"to call the ","type":"text"},{"code":"register(type:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" method.   I like to register all my types at","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"startup, but you can do this at any time after the module has been initialized."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Your module gets initialized on a callback from Godot at different stages, this","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"is how I register my types:"}]},{"type":"codeListing","syntax":"swift","code":["\/\/\/ This method will be invoked at different stages of the execution,","\/\/\/ in our example, we register the type when the level being used is","\/\/\/ `.scene`.","","func setupScene (level: GDExtension.InitializationLevel) {","    if level == .scene {","        register(type: SwiftSprite.self)","    }","}","","\/\/\/ This is the entry point referenced from the `.gdextension` file","\/\/\/ that you used to declare the Swift extension:","@_cdecl(\"swift_entry_point\")","public func swift_entry_point(","    interfacePtr: OpaquePointer?,","    libraryPtr: OpaquePointer?,","    extensionPtr: OpaquePointer?) -> UInt8","{","    print (\"SwiftSprite: Starting up\")","    guard let interfacePtr, let libraryPtr, let extensionPtr else {","        return 0","    }","    initializeSwiftModule(interfacePtr, libraryPtr, extensionPtr, initHook: setupScene, deInitHook: { x in })","    return 1","}"]},{"level":2,"type":"heading","anchor":"Defining-your-class-behavior","text":"Defining your class behavior"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"To make your custom type interesting, you will want to alter the default","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"behavior that Godot provides.   In Godot, this is achieved by overwriting one or","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"more of the virtual methods available in a class."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"In Godot, methods that can be overwritten start with an underscore, like"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"_ready"},{"type":"text","text":" for example."}]},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"While this would not have been my choice desining the API, I find that there is"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"enough external documentation in the Godot  world, that I decided to stick to","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"it.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"In the example shown above, we overwrote the method "},{"code":"_process","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" which is invoked","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"continously by Godot and we use it to change the position of our sprite.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"anchor":"Surfacing-Methods-Properties-and-Signals","level":2,"type":"heading","text":"Surfacing Methods, Properties and Signals"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"In addition to modifying the behavior of a built-in type, you might want to"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"surface properties that would allow users to customize your type from the Godot"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"editor, or expose methods that can be invoked by users from either other","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"programming languages, like GDScript or C#, and you might want to surface","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/Signals","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"type":"text","text":" that your object emits that can be wired up externally."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"To do this, you will be using the ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Godot"},{"text":" macro to annotate your class, like","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"this:","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"syntax":"swift","code":["@Godot","class SwiftSprite: Sprite2D {","}"],"type":"codeListing"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"When you use the "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Godot"},{"text":" macro, a number of additional macros can be used inside","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"your class, like ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Signal"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"reference","identifier":"Signals.md","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Callable"},{"type":"text","text":" to surface a method to"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"Godot, and ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Export"},{"text":" to ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"Exports.md"},{"text":".","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Behind the scenes these macros use the lower-level "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/ClassDB","isActive":true},{"text":" API to define functions,","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"properties and their values.","type":"text"}]},{"level":2,"text":"Overriding Methods","anchor":"Overriding-Methods","type":"heading"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"The Godot Object model does not surface a traditional object-oriented system.","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"Not all the methods surfaced by the Godot API can be overwritten.  The","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"SwiftGodot binding makes this explicit.   Methods that can be overwritten are","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"declared as "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"open"},{"type":"text","text":", while those that can not be overwritten are declared as"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"public","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Godot prefixes all of the overwritable methods with an underscore (we have seen"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"in this guide some examples already, like "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"_process"},{"type":"text","text":")."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Another important difference is that Godot does not expect your code to call the","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"“super” method, in fact, those methods do nothing in SwiftGodot."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Of course, if your Swift code relies on a class hierarhcy where you do delegate"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"code to the original implementation, you should still call the base class","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"method.   For example:"}]},{"code":["@Godot ","class Base: Node2D {","    override func _ready () { ","        \/* important work *\/ ","        \/* no need to call super._ready *\/","    }","}","","class Derived: Base {","    override func _ready () {","        \/* some prep work here *\/","        \/* because we want to execute the Base._ready important work *\/","        super._ready ()","        \/* some additional work here *\/","    }","}"],"type":"codeListing","syntax":"swift"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"You can think of those methods that can be overwritten as hooks into Godot, but","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"with an important distinction.   When you override those methods, Godot knows","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"that you overwrote them, and might take a different course of action based on it."}]},{"type":"heading","text":"Surfacing Methods","anchor":"Surfacing-Methods","level":3},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"To surface a method, apply the "},{"code":"@Callable","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" attribute to it, this will register"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"the method with Godot.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"The only limitation is that the parameters of those methods need to be one","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"of the types that Godot can surface to the rest of the engine: anything that can","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"be passed in a ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/Variant"},{"text":".","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"syntax":"swift","code":["@Callable","func readyCallback (text: String) {","    print (\"readyCallback method called with value: \\(text)\")","    return nil","}"],"type":"codeListing"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Now your method can be invoked from the Godot editor or from scripts written in other languages.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"The functions can be any of the types that can be wrapped in a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"Variant.md"},{"type":"text","text":" including the core Swift data types for integers and"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"floats, the Godot Object subclasses as well as ","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/TypedArray","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/TypedDictionary"},{"text":".","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The "},{"code":"@Callable","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" macro only works in your class definition, and will not work"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"on Swift class extensions."}]},{"text":"Surfacing Properties and Variables","level":3,"type":"heading","anchor":"Surfacing-Properties-and-Variables"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"To surface properties and variables, use the ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Export"},{"type":"text","text":" attribute on them."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"The simplest use case works like this:","type":"text"}]},{"syntax":"swift","code":["@Godot","class Demo: Node3D {","    @Export var greeting: String = 0","}"],"type":"codeListing"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The above code surfaces a property called "},{"code":"greeting","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" to Godot of the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"type string.  Strings and numbers can be edited in many interesting","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"ways in the Godot editor, and that is exposed via two optional parameters","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"to the ","type":"text"},{"code":"Export","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" attribute: a "},{"isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/PropertyHint","type":"reference"},{"type":"text","text":" and sometimes those hints"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"can take an optional string with additional confirmation information.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"To learn more, read the "},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/Exports"},{"text":" page.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","anchor":"Signals","level":3,"text":"Signals"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Surfacing signals is covered in the ","type":"text"},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/Signals","isActive":true},{"text":" document.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"heading","anchor":"Low-Leve-Details-PropInfo","text":"Low-Level Details: PropInfo","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"In SwiftGodot, the ","type":"text"},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/PropInfo"},{"type":"text","text":" structure is used to define argument types,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"properties and return values.  You will be exposed to these when you define","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"signal parameters."}]},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"This is only required if you do not use the various macros provided by"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"SwiftGodot."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"It looks like this:","type":"text"}]},{"syntax":"swift","code":["let textArgument =  PropInfo(","    propertyType: .string,","    propertyName: StringName (\"myArg\"),","    className: \"SwiftSprite\",","    hint: .typeString,","    hintStr: \"\", ","    usage: .default)"],"type":"codeListing"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"In this case, we are declaring a property of the Godot type string, and we call","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"this ","type":"text"},{"code":"myArg","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":".  The ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"className"},{"type":"text","text":" is the name of the class that we are defining it,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"and we can provide additional hints as to how this might be used (The Godot","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"editor can provide custom UI Editors based on this information).","type":"text"}]}],"kind":"content"}],"schemaVersion":{"patch":0,"major":0,"minor":3},"kind":"article","abstract":[{"text":"You will typically extend the functionality of your Godot game by creating","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"classes that derive from one of the various Godot types and adjust the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"behavior accordingly.","type":"text"}],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot"]]},"seeAlsoSections":[{"identifiers":["doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/Differences","doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/Variants","doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/Exports","doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/Signals","doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/BindingNodes","doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/RunningInEditor","doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/MemoryManagement","doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/TypesTranslation"],"title":"Articles and Tutorials","anchor":"Articles-and-Tutorials","generated":true}],"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/swiftgodot\/customtypes"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"metadata":{"title":"Using Custom Types","modules":[{"name":"SwiftGodot"}],"roleHeading":"Article","role":"article"},"sections":[],"identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/CustomTypes"},"references":{"doc://SwiftGodot/documentation/SwiftGodot":{"role":"collection","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot","url":"\/documentation\/swiftgodot","title":"SwiftGodot","kind":"symbol","abstract":[{"text":"Framework to write Godot Game Extensions using the Swift Programming Language.","type":"text"}],"type":"topic"},"doc://SwiftGodot/documentation/SwiftGodot/TypedDictionary":{"url":"\/documentation\/swiftgodot\/typeddictionary","kind":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/TypedDictionary","type":"topic","title":"TypedDictionary","role":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"TypedDictionary","kind":"identifier"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"TypedDictionary"}],"abstract":[{"text":"This type represents typed Godot dictionary, such as ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Dictionary[int, String]"},{"text":" or ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Dictionary[String, Object]"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},"Variant.md":{"url":"Variant.md","identifier":"Variant.md","title":"Variant","type":"link","titleInlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Variant"}]},"doc://SwiftGodot/documentation/SwiftGodot/RunningInEditor":{"kind":"article","type":"topic","title":"Running Code in the Godot Editor","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/RunningInEditor","url":"\/documentation\/swiftgodot\/runningineditor","role":"article","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"There are cases where you might want some of your Swift code for your extension"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"to run while it is being used in the Godot Editor.","type":"text"}]},"doc://SwiftGodot/documentation/SwiftGodot/Exports":{"kind":"article","type":"topic","title":"Exports","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/Exports","url":"\/documentation\/swiftgodot\/exports","role":"article","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"In Godot, class members can be exported. This means their value gets saved along"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"with the resource (such as the scene) they’re attached to. They will also be","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"available for editing in the property editor. Exporting is done by using the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Export"},{"text":" annotation.","type":"text"}]},"doc://SwiftGodot/documentation/SwiftGodot/Differences":{"kind":"article","type":"topic","title":"SwiftGodot API differences to GDScript","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/Differences","url":"\/documentation\/swiftgodot\/differences","role":"article","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"This document lists some common differences between GDSscript and SwiftGodot."}]},"doc://SwiftGodot/documentation/SwiftGodot/TypesTranslation":{"url":"\/documentation\/swiftgodot\/typestranslation","title":"Types Translation","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/TypesTranslation","abstract":[{"text":"This document contains information on how type translation happens between Swift and Godot.","type":"text"}],"role":"article","type":"topic","kind":"article"},"doc://SwiftGodot/documentation/SwiftGodot/Variant":{"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Variant objects box various Godot Objects, you create them with one of the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"constructors, and you can retrieve the contents using the various extension"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"constructors that are declared on the various types that are wrapped."}],"url":"\/documentation\/swiftgodot\/variant","kind":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/Variant","role":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"class"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"kind":"identifier","text":"Variant"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"Variant"}],"type":"topic","title":"Variant"},"Exports.md":{"type":"link","url":"Exports.md","titleInlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"surface properties"}],"title":"surface properties","identifier":"Exports.md"},"Signals.md":{"url":"Signals.md","identifier":"Signals.md","title":"to define signals","type":"link","titleInlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"to define signals"}]},"doc://SwiftGodot/documentation/SwiftGodot/Variants":{"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Follow up on the fundamental building block of Godot’s data types."}],"kind":"article","url":"\/documentation\/swiftgodot\/variants","type":"topic","title":"Variants","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/Variants","role":"article"},"doc://SwiftGodot/documentation/SwiftGodot/BindingNodes":{"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"You will find yourself referencing nodes from a scene in your code. In"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"GDScript, that is usually achieved by using the dollar sign and the name of the","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"object you want to reference."}],"kind":"article","url":"\/documentation\/swiftgodot\/bindingnodes","type":"topic","title":"Referencing Nodes from your Scene","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/BindingNodes","role":"article"},"doc://SwiftGodot/documentation/SwiftGodot/ClassDB":{"title":"ClassDB","kind":"symbol","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A class information repository."}],"fragments":[{"text":"class","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"kind":"identifier","text":"ClassDB"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"ClassDB"}],"type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/ClassDB","url":"\/documentation\/swiftgodot\/classdb","role":"symbol"},"doc://SwiftGodot/documentation/SwiftGodot/PropertyHint":{"navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"PropertyHint"}],"role":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/swiftgodot\/propertyhint","title":"PropertyHint","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"enum"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"text":"PropertyHint","kind":"identifier"}],"kind":"symbol","type":"topic","abstract":[],"identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/PropertyHint"},"doc://SwiftGodot/documentation/SwiftGodot/MemoryManagement":{"kind":"article","type":"topic","title":"Memory Management in SwiftGodot","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/MemoryManagement","url":"\/documentation\/swiftgodot\/memorymanagement","role":"article","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"SwiftGodot surfaces various Godot types into the Swift universe in various ways:"}]},"doc://SwiftGodot/documentation/SwiftGodot/TypedArray":{"fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"TypedArray"}],"abstract":[{"text":"This type represents typed Godot array, such as ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Array[int]"},{"type":"text","text":" or "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Array[Object]"},{"type":"text","text":"."}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/TypedArray","title":"TypedArray","kind":"symbol","type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/swiftgodot\/typedarray","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"TypedArray","kind":"identifier"}],"role":"symbol"},"doc://SwiftGodot/documentation/SwiftGodot/PropInfo":{"url":"\/documentation\/swiftgodot\/propinfo","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/PropInfo","title":"PropInfo","abstract":[{"text":"PropInfo structures describe arguments to signals, and methods as well as return values from methods.","type":"text"}],"type":"topic","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"PropInfo"}],"kind":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"text":"PropInfo","kind":"identifier"}],"role":"symbol"},"doc://SwiftGodot/documentation/SwiftGodot/Signals":{"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Signals in Godot are used by objects to post interesting events that are"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"taking place, and can be used by users to easily add behavior and react","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"to changes."}],"kind":"article","url":"\/documentation\/swiftgodot\/signals","type":"topic","title":"Signals","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/Signals","role":"article"}}}
